WEBVTT

00:00:00.201 --> 00:00:02.169
Here's that car on the flat roundabout.

00:00:02.751 --> 00:00:04.676
To go round, it needs a net force

00:00:04.778 --> 00:00:06.001
pointing towards the centre.

00:00:06.623 --> 00:00:08.688
Its weight pulls down, and the road pushes

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:10.814
up, so neither of those can do it.

00:00:11.417 --> 00:00:13.484
So which force is pulling the car inwards?

00:00:14.208 --> 00:00:16.598
Is it air resistance, friction from the road,

00:00:17.101 --> 00:00:18.748
or a separate centripetal force?

00:00:19.416 --> 00:00:20.064
Make a prediction.

00:00:24.337 --> 00:00:26.103
Let's look at the car from behind, as

00:00:26.144 --> 00:00:28.171
it drives into the screen, with the centre

00:00:28.192 --> 00:00:29.275
of the bend on the left.

00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:31.013
Its weight acts straight down.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:33.709
The road pushes straight up with a normal

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:35.938
force, the push of a surface at right

00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:36.501
angles to it.

00:00:37.144 --> 00:00:39.245
The car isn't moving up or down, so

00:00:39.306 --> 00:00:42.067
these two forces balance, and N equals m

00:00:42.128 --> 00:00:42.347
g.

00:00:43.309 --> 00:00:46.371
Air resistance pushes backwards along the path, against

00:00:46.412 --> 00:00:48.853
the motion, so it doesn't point inwards either.

00:00:49.489 --> 00:00:52.201
That leaves friction, the grip between the tyres

00:00:52.282 --> 00:00:52.864
and the road.

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:55.365
Think about what the car would do without

00:00:55.386 --> 00:00:55.446
it.

00:00:56.211 --> 00:00:58.518
From the last video, with no inward force

00:00:58.860 --> 00:01:01.047
it would carry straight on along the tangent.

00:01:01.590 --> 00:01:03.835
So compared with the curving road, the tyres

00:01:03.876 --> 00:01:05.340
would start to slide outwards.

00:01:06.062 --> 00:01:08.627
Friction opposes that sliding, so it pushes on

00:01:08.668 --> 00:01:10.731
the tyres inwards, towards the centre.

00:01:11.474 --> 00:01:13.719
The tyres grip rather than slide, so this

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:14.802
is static friction.

00:01:15.667 --> 00:01:17.033
So the answer is friction.

00:01:17.457 --> 00:01:19.250
There's no separate centripetal force.

00:01:19.776 --> 00:01:21.672
Friction is doing the centripetal job.

00:01:22.309 --> 00:01:24.722
So in this rear view there are three

00:01:24.803 --> 00:01:25.587
forces to draw.

00:01:26.251 --> 00:01:29.318
Along the path, the engine's drive balances air

00:01:29.359 --> 00:01:31.303
resistance, so we leave that pair out.

00:01:32.109 --> 00:01:34.685
Vertically, the normal force balances the weight.

00:01:35.469 --> 00:01:38.615
Sideways, friction is the only force, so it's

00:01:38.657 --> 00:01:40.560
the whole net force towards the centre.

00:01:41.224 --> 00:01:42.990
That gives us the key equation for a

00:01:43.091 --> 00:01:43.713
flat curve.

00:01:44.256 --> 00:01:46.885
Friction equals m v squared over r.

00:01:47.473 --> 00:01:48.043
Let's try it.

00:01:48.649 --> 00:01:51.053
A twelve hundred kilogram car rounds a flat

00:01:51.134 --> 00:01:54.480
curve of radius forty metres at fifteen metres

00:01:54.521 --> 00:01:55.001
per second.

00:01:55.666 --> 00:01:56.736
How much friction does it need?

00:01:57.604 --> 00:02:00.045
Friction equals m v squared over r,

00:02:00.566 --> 00:02:03.927
so that's twelve hundred times fifteen squared, over

00:02:04.008 --> 00:02:04.368
forty.

00:02:05.070 --> 00:02:07.958
That's six thousand seven hundred and fifty newtons,

00:02:08.260 --> 00:02:09.101
towards the centre.

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:11.099
That's the friction the turn needs.

00:02:11.564 --> 00:02:13.173
It isn't the most friction the road could

00:02:13.214 --> 00:02:14.884
give, and that difference matters.

00:02:15.542 --> 00:02:17.937
Static friction can only grow so far.

00:02:18.561 --> 00:02:21.283
Its maximum is mu s times N,

00:02:21.903 --> 00:02:24.825
where mu s, the coefficient of static friction,

00:02:25.285 --> 00:02:27.326
is a number that describes how well the

00:02:27.366 --> 00:02:28.387
two surfaces grip.

00:02:29.248 --> 00:02:29.930
On a flat road

00:02:30.492 --> 00:02:31.675
N is m g,

00:02:32.238 --> 00:02:34.908
so the most friction available is mu s

00:02:35.169 --> 00:02:35.510
m g.

00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:37.916
The car is at its top speed when

00:02:37.937 --> 00:02:40.522
the friction it needs just equals the most

00:02:40.663 --> 00:02:41.263
that's available.

00:02:41.866 --> 00:02:42.065
So

00:02:42.708 --> 00:02:45.634
m v squared over r equals mu s

00:02:46.076 --> 00:02:46.416
m g.

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.749
The mass cancels, and v max equals the

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:52.139
square root of mu s g r.

00:02:52.861 --> 00:02:55.585
For a flat curve of radius thirty metres,

00:02:56.026 --> 00:02:58.410
with mu s equal to zero point six

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:58.791
zero,

00:02:59.312 --> 00:03:01.515
v max is the square root of zero

00:03:01.555 --> 00:03:04.300
point six zero times nine point eight times

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:04.761
thirty.

00:03:05.382 --> 00:03:07.086
That's the square root of one hundred and

00:03:07.146 --> 00:03:10.133
seventy-six point four, about thirteen point three metres

00:03:10.153 --> 00:03:12.979
per second, or about forty-eight kilometres per hour.

00:03:13.749 --> 00:03:15.117
Look at what's in that formula,

00:03:15.621 --> 00:03:16.364
and what isn't.

00:03:17.089 --> 00:03:20.094
The mass cancelled, so a loaded truck and

00:03:20.115 --> 00:03:22.358
a small car have the same top speed

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:24.603
on the same bend, with the same grip.

00:03:25.547 --> 00:03:27.353
A bigger radius gives a higher limit.

00:03:28.117 --> 00:03:30.206
And on a wet road mu s drops.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:32.936
If it halved to zero point three zero,

00:03:33.399 --> 00:03:35.345
v max would fall to about nine point

00:03:35.426 --> 00:03:36.409
four metres per second.

00:03:37.232 --> 00:03:40.259
Remember, the friction needed rises with the square

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:40.880
of the speed,

00:03:41.502 --> 00:03:43.025
so the margin disappears quickly.

00:03:43.788 --> 00:03:46.295
So what happens if the car goes faster

00:03:46.456 --> 00:03:47.178
than v max?

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:50.161
The road can't supply enough friction, so the

00:03:50.222 --> 00:03:52.843
net inward force is smaller than m v

00:03:52.904 --> 00:03:53.904
squared over r.

00:03:54.626 --> 00:03:57.073
The car still turns, but not as sharply

00:03:57.214 --> 00:03:57.735
as the bend.

00:03:58.398 --> 00:04:00.564
Its path is a wider curve, and it

00:04:00.645 --> 00:04:03.131
drifts towards the outside of the roundabout.

00:04:03.776 --> 00:04:05.290
Nothing throws it outwards.

00:04:05.755 --> 00:04:07.944
The inward force just isn't big enough.

00:04:08.534 --> 00:04:10.996
A common mistake is drawing an extra arrow

00:04:11.077 --> 00:04:13.618
labelled centripetal force, next to the friction.

00:04:14.321 --> 00:04:16.296
That counts the same force twice.

00:04:17.228 --> 00:04:18.182
Here's a way to remember it.

00:04:18.808 --> 00:04:21.223
Centripetal is a job, not a force.

00:04:21.948 --> 00:04:24.810
Draw the real forces first, then ask which

00:04:24.851 --> 00:04:26.572
of them does the job of pointing to

00:04:26.613 --> 00:04:27.073
the centre.

00:04:29.641 --> 00:04:30.071
Your turn.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:33.285
A twelve hundred kilogram car travels around a

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:36.777
flat, unbanked bend of radius forty-five metres.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:40.162
The coefficient of static friction between its tyres

00:04:40.243 --> 00:04:42.544
and the road is zero point six five.

00:04:43.506 --> 00:04:46.311
Identify the force that provides the centripetal force,

00:04:46.913 --> 00:04:49.297
and calculate the greatest speed at which the

00:04:49.318 --> 00:04:50.440
car can take the bend.

00:04:51.223 --> 00:04:52.047
Pause here to answer.

00:05:03.201 --> 00:05:05.228
On a flat bend, static friction is the

00:05:05.489 --> 00:05:08.902
only horizontal force, so it provides the centripetal

00:05:08.962 --> 00:05:09.263
force.

00:05:10.066 --> 00:05:10.548
At the limit,

00:05:11.090 --> 00:05:13.620
m v squared over r equals mu s

00:05:13.841 --> 00:05:15.588
m g, and the mass cancels.

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.033
So v max is the square root of

00:05:18.093 --> 00:05:20.438
zero point six five times nine point eight

00:05:20.618 --> 00:05:21.519
times forty-five,

00:05:22.101 --> 00:05:24.104
about sixteen point nine metres per second.

00:05:24.766 --> 00:05:26.851
The classic slip is forgetting the square root,

00:05:27.092 --> 00:05:29.437
and getting an impossible two hundred and eighty-seven

00:05:29.478 --> 00:05:30.159
metres per second.

00:05:31.904 --> 00:05:32.329
Last one.

00:05:33.341 --> 00:05:35.988
A fifteen hundred kilogram car enters a flat

00:05:36.048 --> 00:05:39.677
roundabout of radius fifteen metres at thirty-five kilometres

00:05:39.717 --> 00:05:40.058
per hour.

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.945
The road is wet, and the coefficient of

00:05:43.026 --> 00:05:45.432
static friction is zero point four zero.

00:05:46.174 --> 00:05:47.900
Determine whether the car can get round at

00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:49.626
this speed without sliding.

00:05:50.452 --> 00:05:51.277
Pause here to answer.

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:02.621
First convert.

00:06:03.149 --> 00:06:05.840
Thirty-five kilometres per hour is about nine point

00:06:05.921 --> 00:06:07.447
seven two metres per second.

00:06:08.191 --> 00:06:10.636
The friction needed is m v squared over

00:06:10.897 --> 00:06:13.382
r, about nine thousand four hundred and fifty

00:06:13.423 --> 00:06:13.762
newtons.

00:06:14.685 --> 00:06:16.507
The most the wet road can give is

00:06:16.749 --> 00:06:19.693
zero point four zero times fifteen hundred times

00:06:19.714 --> 00:06:22.337
nine point eight, which is five thousand eight

00:06:22.398 --> 00:06:23.379
hundred and eighty newtons.

00:06:24.122 --> 00:06:26.170
That's not enough, so the car slides.

00:06:27.154 --> 00:06:29.299
The slip is putting thirty-five straight into the

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:31.525
formula, without converting to metres per second.

00:06:33.388 --> 00:06:36.014
So on a flat road, friction does the

00:06:36.256 --> 00:06:37.037
inward job.

00:06:37.862 --> 00:06:38.997
Now take the road away.

00:06:39.621 --> 00:06:41.962
Hang a small steel ball on a string

00:06:42.043 --> 00:06:44.284
from a fixed support, and set it going

00:06:44.365 --> 00:06:46.065
round in a horizontal circle.

00:06:46.928 --> 00:06:49.754
Only two forces act now, the string's pull

00:06:50.176 --> 00:06:50.777
and gravity.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:53.369
And the string doesn't stay horizontal.

00:06:53.692 --> 00:06:55.520
It slants, tracing out a cone.

00:06:56.204 --> 00:06:59.257
So how does one slanted string hold the

00:06:59.278 --> 00:07:00.984
ball up, and pull it inwards?

00:07:03.795 --> 00:07:04.617
Here are the key notes.

00:07:05.141 --> 00:07:06.688
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:06.729 --> 00:07:07.150
to copy them.
